{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "Our main living systematic review of interventions is collecting results from randomised or non-randomised trials of mental health interventions conducted in any population during COVID-19. ... Eligible interventions included any intervention described as designed to address COVID-19 mental health challenges or primarily addressing mental health symptoms from COVID-19. ... Eligible comparators included: (1) inactive control conditions (e.g., no treatment, waitlist) and (2) other eligible interventions. Eligible outcomes were de fi ned broadly and included general mental health, mental health quality of life, anxiety symptoms, depression symptoms, stress, loneliness, anger, grief, burnout, and other emotional states. To be eligible, trials had to report outcomes collected at least 1 week after intervention initiation and include at least 10 total participants. ... The present report focuses on a subset of psychological interventions from the main review that were designed to address COVID-19 mental health among people not known to have COVID-19. Thus, it does not include trials done exclusively with hospitalised patients or persons quarantined due to COVID-19 infections or exposure... we excluded trials of brief single-session interventions (e.g., 30 min) with no subsequent follow-up, and we excluded non-randomised studies.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies the study designs (randomised controlled trials), population (people not quarantined or undergoing treatment due to COVID-19), interventions (mental health interventions), comparators, outcomes, and follow-up requirements (at least 1 week after intervention initiation)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "There were no restrictions on language or publication format.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states there were no restrictions on language or publication format. While dissemination year is not explicitly stated as a restriction, the search dates are provided elsewhere (Dec 31, 2019 to March 22, 2021), covering the relevant period."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not report excluding studies specifically because outcomes were not measured versus not reported; it focuses on study design and verification status for inclusion."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_13",
      "quote": "Of the 9 included trials, 3 trials 30-32 tested interventions designed speci fi cally to address mental health challenges in COVID-19, and 6 33-38 tested standard interventions that were only minimally adapted or not adapted for COVID-19.",
      "reason": "The review groups the included studies into two categories (those specifically designed for COVID-19 mental health challenges vs. standard interventions) for the purpose of descriptive synthesis."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "We did not include nonrandomised studies because such studies are highly prone to bias when intervention and control groups are self-selected or there is no control group. ... Additionally, the high volume of poor-quality research being published on COVID-19 is a barrier to synthesis, 26 and we encountered many trials that were of extremely poor quality, of unclear origin and sponsorship, and reported effect sizes that, in some cases, exceeded plausibility. Thus, we contacted the authors of all included studies by email up to 2 times and requested that they verify the authenticity of published methods and results and con fi rm the accuracy of our extracted data.",
      "reason": "The authors provide clear rationales for excluding non-randomised studies (bias) and for requiring author verification of study data (poor quality/implausible results in COVID-19 literature)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

